  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 309 AM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nThe Long-Term period begins with a ridge centered over the \nMississippi Valley region, which will amplify and build eastward, \ngoverning the forecast over the extended period. As it does, \noppressive heat and humidity will ensue from Friday through the \nbeginning of next week, with high temperatures building into the mid \nto upper 90s over most of the CWA, outside of the far northeastern \nmountains. These higher temperatures, in combination with \nprecipitable water values ranging from 1.8-2.3 inches and dew points \nin the upper 70s, will create unsafe afternoon conditions. As a \nresult, heat index values in the 105-110F range are expected in the \ncentral and eastern counties by Friday and across most of the CWA by \nthe start of the weekend, likely warranting the issuance of a Heat \nAdvisory each day. \n\nThe suppressive high-pressure conditions should support a reduction \nin precipitation chances through the beginning of next week, with \nmodels showing diurnal thunderstorm chances dwindling to isolated \ncoverage as we move into the weekend. One caveat is the continuation \nof northwest flow, at least through Friday, followed by any \ndisturbances that may transit the periphery of the ridge, which \ncould enhance or trigger a few more afternoon thunderstorms than \ncurrently expected. If so, some of these stronger storms could",
